A||||5||OcalaFiero||Distributor Install||||1||
Z||000000||OcalaFiero||08-08-2001||12:35 PM||||I have an '86 4cyl, 5 speed. A few weeks back the car would just "cut off" at idle (usually at a red light). After this happened a few times, she would not restart. There seemed to be no spark or fuel delivery to the injector. I replaced the fuel pump relay, and had the ignition module and coil tested. (The module tested ok, and the person at the parts counter couldn't figure out how to check the coil.) Upon reinstalling the module into the distributor, I noticed that the previous owner had snapped off one of the distributor cap screws in the base of the distributor and upon closer inspection, noticed alot of rust and corrosion in the cap and on the inside parts of the distributor. I went ahead and tried a new pick up coil, and went to reinstall the distributor. It turns out that my boneheaded brother in law didn't properly mark the position of the distributor or rotor when he removed it from the block. I set the #1 cylinder to TDC and my manual states the the rotor should be placed to the point between #1 & #3 terminal. I did this and had problems getting her to turn over. I advanced the distributor and she eventually started up and ran great. After a test drive, I was unable to restart the car. I am going to pick up a new distributor tonight and want to get input as to the proper procedure for installing it after the camshaft has moved. i.e. the procedure that somebody out there has used to set the motor and distributor at TDC. The Haynes manual says to fell for compression on number 1 and continue turning the crankshaft until the timing mark is at "0", no problem there. Then the manual says to install the distributor with the rotor between #1 and #3 terminals, shouldn't it be pointing to number 1 directly? Also if you can think of any other problems that may be contributing to the stalling/ non starting issue I'd appreceate it. Thanks in advance.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||08-08-2001||12:57 PM||||Follow the instructions you have with the following exception:<P>Feel for compression of #1 cylinder. Stop turning the crankshaft when timing mark comes to (?)* BTC (use your info on deck lid)<BR>and drop the distributor in with the rotor pointing directly at #1 wire post. Tighten retaining bolt, start engine and check timing.<P>This SHOULD cure your problems.||||reg||1
Z||000002||OcalaFiero||08-08-2001||01:06 PM||||When I put the distributor into the block with the rotor pointed at #1 terminal, it should be around the 10 or 11 o'clock point, correct? and the Teeth or points that stick up from the pickup coil area should line up with the points on the distributor shaft, correct?<P>    {..) {....}  &lt;(connectors)<P>     1  |  3<BR>     _  +  _<BR>     2  |||38.200.25.106||reg||1
Z||000003||OcalaFiero||08-17-2001||09:17 AM||||After Numerous evenings and weekends of frustration, my little red head is running again, sort of. In all the computer, coil, fuel pump, oil pressure sender and distributor were replaced. The oil pressure switch and distributor were definately bad, the other things we easy money for autozone, but probably would have needed replacement at some point anyway. So after countless tries at getting the base timin right, I noticed that it seemed the injector wasnt pumping out quite enough fuel. I checked the relay for the fuel pump, and lo and behold, that lovely green "muck" in the pigtail that the fuel pump relay plugs into. So it'll be off to the junkyard this weekend to find a new one. Thanks for the help through out this diagnostic adventure.||||reg||6
Z||000004||Indiana_resto_guy||08-17-2001||09:35 AM||||You could try some electrical contact cleaner on it. If you can clean it and it works coat contacts with dielectric compound.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Bluebottlejunky||08-17-2001||12:02 PM||||Did you make sure you ground out the computer? so its setting the base timing?<P>------------------<BR>Mike<BR>85'GT 4sp<BR>AIM: <B>KilleRedGT</B>||||reg||1
